Overview
I worked as the Data Modeler in this project, with a primary focus on designing the relational structure and building reliable seed/data insert scripts. My role was to make sure the database schema supports business requirements and that the inserted data is realistic, consistent, and ready for analytics and reporting.

Data Modeling Contribution
In the modeling phase, I focused on creating a clean and scalable structure:

- Defined core entities and relationships based on project requirements.
- Established primary keys and foreign keys to enforce referential integrity.
- Designed normalized table structures to reduce redundancy and improve consistency.
- Mapped business concepts (customers, products, orders, sales reps, returns, quotas, regions) into relational tables.
- Ensured compatibility between transactional tables and later analytical/reporting needs.

Seeding and Data Insert Responsibilities
My main responsibility was preparing and managing seed scripts for initial and testing datasets:

- Created insert scripts for dimension and transactional tables in a logical load order.
- Populated data with realistic values to simulate real business behavior.
- Ensured parent-child inserts follow foreign key dependencies to avoid constraint errors.
- Added enough data variety (countries, categories, customer tiers, statuses, dates) to support complex SQL queries.
- Balanced sample volume to enable KPI calculations, joins, and advanced analytical queries.

Data Quality and Validation
To ensure the dataset was stable and usable, I performed consistency checks:

- Verified foreign key links after inserts.
- Checked for duplicates in key business entities where uniqueness was expected.
- Reviewed nullability and required fields to avoid incomplete records.
- Validated date ranges and numeric values for business logic correctness.
- Re-tested insert scripts after schema adjustments to keep data aligned with model updates.

Impact on Project
The data modeling and seeding work provided the foundation for later phases:

- Enabled query development and KPI calculations with complete and connected datasets.
- Reduced debugging time in analytical phases by preventing data integrity issues early.
- Supported team members by delivering a dependable dataset for business queries and reporting.

Key Improvements
- Improved insert sequencing to fully respect relational dependencies.
- Refined sample data coverage to better support edge cases in SQL tasks.
- Standardized seed structure for easier maintenance and repeatable setup.

Peer Review
- Query reviewed - Month-over-Month Revenue:
This query is well structured and correctly uses `LAG` for trend comparison by country. A small improvement would be adding `NULLIF` in the percentage calculation to avoid division-by-zero when previous month revenue is 0.

- Query reviewed - Q2 Pending/Partially Delivered Orders:
This query answers the business question clearly and applies good filtering for status and latest Q3. It can be improved by using `li.net_price` (or discounted value) instead of `quantity * unit_price` so totals stay consistent with net revenue logic used in the rest of the project.
